WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.050
I 
think lot of people maybe go through life
without really noticing all of the things

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.239
around them and as Course 4’s, we are very
aware of the world around us, and I think

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:38.210
we learn from it, and think of how we can
make the world a better place.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.810
The magical thing about Course 4 is it really
teaches you how to take your creativity and

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:52.200
design ideas and then translate them into
physical realities that are spatial, occupiable,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.300
inhabitable and have kind of powerful cultural
resonance.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:14.570
I think that all of these different interests
come together in the studio environment; that’s

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.780
really where all of our different ideas get
to take form.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.969
Just about all of our studios have a lot of
freedom in our ability to design our own projects

00:01:24.969 --> 00:01:27.780
and take our own ideas as far as we can.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:31.119
You see other people’s projects and you’re
like ‘how did they come up with that?’;

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:34.310
‘how did that mind come up with something
that cool.’

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:40.659
It is a costume for the Galactic Olympics
and it is basically supposed to connect every

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:43.619
part of your body.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:48.600
The goal is develop new structural systems
using small diameter round timber.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.060
We’re currently on the site of the Collier
Memorial, a structure that is being built

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.310
in Sean Collier’s honor.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:03.490
The basic concept of the project is that it
stands as a long-lasting monument that speaks

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:08.039
to the strength of our community and we designed
it to work in compression, as a series of

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.280
stones to be held in place only by gravity.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.290
As an undergrad we’re only used to seeing
small models, but actually blowing it up to

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:23.400
real scale where people can actually pass
by and inhabit the space, is unbelievable.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:40.420
I specifically really liked the experience
of design reviews, going in front of a group

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.810
of people and having to present your work,
and understanding that that is just part of

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.410
the process and it can better and you can
build.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.070
Course 4 gave me the opportunity to work in
a professional environment and work alongside

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:54.940
extremely talented and motivated people.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:03:01.900
You work so closely with faculty; you talk
so much with professors. You learn something

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:07.090
you can never learn from just taking a class.
The professors learn from you, you learn from

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:07.840
professors.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.600
There are civil engineers, and there are mechanical
engineers, materials scientists and architecture

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.280
majors and everyone plays a role. It makes
a lot of sense because architecture is a lot

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.640
more interdisciplinary than a lot of  people expect.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.320
It’s so cool to get to work with designers
and engineers at the same time; I think that

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.340
really validates the work.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.860
One of the things that I really liked about
MIT was that I was exposed to all different

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:39.160
types of fields; really a great cross-pollination
of disciplines that I think led to what I

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.030
do now.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:46.910
Course 4 brings together architects, designers,
artists, historians, and engineers around

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:53.000
ideas and concepts to transform how we imagine
and construct our environments.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.730
I’ve always thought that the architecture
department teaches you how to solve problems

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:03.830
and its a way of thinking that lets you approach
the unknown.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:10.840
Theres an incredible amount of support. It’s
almost an expectation that you can immediately

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.319
try to make an impact. I think that’s the
really exciting part; it’s not just about

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:19.400
working abstractly, its really about immediately
applying what you’re learning to try to

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:20.500
make the world a better place.

